				<article id="post-1191" class="entry content-bg single-entry post-1191 post type-post status-publish format-standard has-post-thumbnail hentry category-bedroom-storage category-closet-wardrobe">
	<div class="entry-content-wrap">
		
<div class="entry-content single-content">
	
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Learning <strong>how to fold clothes to save space</strong> is the cheapest storage upgrade there is. You do not need new furniture or a bigger closet; you just change the shape of what goes into the drawers you already have. A few simple folds can fit noticeably more into the same dresser, and, just as important, let you see every item at a glance instead of digging through a pile.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This guide walks through 12 easy methods for T-shirts, jeans, sweaters, socks, underwear, towels and sheets, including two ways to fold for a suitcase. Each one takes a minute to learn. Start with the clothes you wear most, and your drawers will look different by the end of one laundry day.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-01-file-folded-tshirt-drawer.jpg" alt="How to fold clothes to save space: T-shirts file-folded upright in neat color-sorted rows inside an open white oak dresser drawer" class="wp-image-1183" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-01-file-folded-tshirt-drawer.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-01-file-folded-tshirt-drawer-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-01-file-folded-tshirt-drawer-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-01-file-folded-tshirt-drawer-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<div class="wp-block-group dsh-summary has-background" style="border-radius:12px;border-left-color:#8FA689;border-left-width:5px;background-color:#EEF3EC;padding-top:20px;padding-right:24px;padding-bottom:20px;padding-left:24px"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-0d2d72cd wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Key Takeaways</strong></p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Quick answer:</strong> To fold clothes to save space, file fold them into small rectangles that stand upright in the drawer instead of stacking them, roll stretchy items like leggings and pajamas, fold socks flat instead of balling them, and use drawer dividers so each row stays in place. For luggage, roll soft clothes tightly or use packing cubes.</li>


<li>Fold each item to roughly the height of the drawer, minus about 1 in (2–3 cm).</li>


<li>Fold only clean, completely dry clothes, especially before seasonal storage.</li>

</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Time:</strong> 1–2 hours per dresser · <strong>Cost:</strong> $0–$30 (£0–£25) for dividers · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Easy</p>


</div></div>


