<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Whatever fold you use, start with clean, dry clothes. Iowa State University Extension advises laundering clothes before you store them, so hidden stains from food or sweat do not show up later, and making sure items are completely dry to prevent mildew. For wool, it recommends storing sweaters clean in an airtight container to protect them from clothes moths (<a href="https://blogs.extension.iastate.edu/answerline/2017/11/13/storing-clothing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Iowa State University Extension: storing clothing</a>).</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Quick Pick: The Best Fold for Each Item</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table class="has-fixed-layout"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Best fold</th><th>Where it goes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>T-shirts and tank tops</td><td>File fold (thirds)</td><td>Top or middle drawer</td></tr><tr><td>Jeans and trousers</td><td>Fold in half, then thirds</td><td>Deep bottom drawer</td></tr><tr><td>Leggings, gym wear, pajamas</td><td>Tight roll</td><td>Drawer or bin</td></tr><tr><td>Sweaters and knits</td><td>Compact rectangle</td><td>Shelf box or wide drawer</td></tr><tr><td>Socks</td><td>Folded flat in pairs</td><td>Divided shallow drawer</td></tr><tr><td>Travel clothes</td><td>Ranger roll or cubes</td><td>Suitcase</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Refold a Whole Dresser in 6 Steps</h2>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li>Empty one drawer at a time onto the bed.</li>


<li>Take out anything that does not fit, is worn out or has not been worn in a year.</li>


<li>Group what is left by type: T-shirts, jeans, sleepwear, socks and so on.</li>


<li>Measure the drawer height so you know how tall each fold should be.</li>


<li>Fold each group using the methods below and file the pieces upright from back to front.</li>


<li>Add a divider or a small box if rows tip over, then repeat with the next drawer.</li>

</ol>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Fold Clothes to Save Space in Drawers</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">These seven methods cover almost everything in a dresser. Practice each one a few times and it becomes automatic.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">1. File fold T-shirts</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Lay the shirt face down and smooth it flat. Fold one side and its sleeve to the middle, then the other side, so you have a long rectangle. Fold it in half lengthwise into a long, narrow strip, then fold it into thirds from the bottom until it stands up on its own. Stand it on its edge in the drawer with the fold facing up, so you can see each shirt like a file in a cabinet.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-02-tshirt-folding-steps.jpg" alt="Sage green T-shirt shown in four folding stages on an oak table: laid flat, sleeves folded in, folded lengthwise and folded into a small upright rectangle" class="wp-image-1184" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-02-tshirt-folding-steps.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-02-tshirt-folding-steps-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-02-tshirt-folding-steps-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-02-tshirt-folding-steps-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">2. Fold long-sleeve tops the same way</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Long-sleeve tops follow the same steps, with one extra move: after folding each side in, fold the sleeve back down along the edge of the rectangle so it does not stick out. Thin knits fold smaller than you expect, so you can often fit two rows in a standard drawer.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">3. Fold jeans and trousers into thirds</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Fold jeans in half lengthwise so one leg lies on the other, tuck in the back seat point, then fold them into thirds from the hem up. They become a firm block that stands upright in a deep drawer. Group them by color or by fit so you can grab the right pair without unfolding a stack.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-03-jeans-deep-drawer.jpg" alt="Jeans and chinos folded into thirds and filed upright in a deep oak dresser drawer with a fabric divider between denim and khaki" class="wp-image-1185" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-03-jeans-deep-drawer.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-03-jeans-deep-drawer-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-03-jeans-deep-drawer-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-03-jeans-deep-drawer-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">4. Roll leggings, gym clothes and pajamas</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Stretchy fabrics slide out of flat folds, so roll them instead. Fold leggings in half lengthwise, then roll from the waist down into a tight cylinder. Pajama sets roll together, top around bottoms, so the matching pieces stay as one bundle.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">5. Fold sweaters into compact rectangles</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Heavy knits can stretch at the shoulders when they hang for months, so most sweaters are better folded. Fold the sleeves across the back, fold the sides in to make a neat column, then fold in half or thirds. Stand them upright in a fabric box on a shelf, or lay a few flat in a wide drawer; for more shelf ideas, see our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-closet-organization-ideas/">small closet organization ideas</a>.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-04-sweaters-fabric-box.jpg" alt="Knit sweaters folded into compact rectangles standing upright in a cream fabric box on an oak shelf, next to a box of folded cardigans" class="wp-image-1186" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-04-sweaters-fabric-box.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-04-sweaters-fabric-box-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-04-sweaters-fabric-box-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-04-sweaters-fabric-box-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">6. Fold socks flat instead of balling them</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Balling socks by turning the cuffs over each other stretches the elastic. Lay the pair on top of each other and fold into halves or thirds, depending on length, so they form a small flat square. They file neatly into a divided drawer and keep their elastic longer.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">7. Roll underwear and nest bras</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Fold underwear in thirds lengthwise and roll it into a small cylinder, or fold it into a tiny square. Do not fold molded bras in half; nest them one inside the other in a row so the cups keep their shape. A honeycomb divider gives each item a home and stops the drawer from turning into a tangle.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-05-socks-underwear-honeycomb.jpg" alt="Shallow drawer with a white honeycomb divider holding socks folded flat and rolled underwear, one item per cell" class="wp-image-1187" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-05-socks-underwear-honeycomb.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-05-socks-underwear-honeycomb-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-05-socks-underwear-honeycomb-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-05-socks-underwear-honeycomb-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Fold Towels and Bedding to Save Space</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">8. Fold towels in thirds, then roll or fold</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Fold bath towels in thirds lengthwise so the side edges are hidden, then fold into thirds again for a shelf, or roll them for a basket. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/linen-closet-organization/">linen closet organization</a> guide shows how to fit towels and sheets on the same set of shelves.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">9. Fold a fitted sheet and store sets in a pillowcase</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Tuck the corners of the fitted sheet into each other so the elastic edges line up, lay it flat, and fold it into a rectangle, then a square. Fold the flat sheet and one pillowcase to the same size, and slide the whole set into the second pillowcase. One bundle per bed makes changing the sheets much faster.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-06-fitted-sheet-bundle.jpg" alt="Fitted sheet folded into a neat square next to a sheet set stored inside its matching pillowcase on an oak linen shelf" class="wp-image-1188" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-06-fitted-sheet-bundle.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-06-fitted-sheet-bundle-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-06-fitted-sheet-bundle-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-06-fitted-sheet-bundle-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Fold Clothes to Save Space in Luggage</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">10. Use the ranger roll for soft clothes</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The ranger roll, sometimes called the army roll, packs T-shirts, leggings and pajamas into tight bundles that do not unroll. Fold the bottom hem up about 3 in (8 cm) inside out to make a pocket, fold the sides in, roll tightly from the collar down, then flip the pocket over the roll to lock it. Rolled clothes fill the gaps in a suitcase and wrinkle less than loose folds.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-07-rolled-clothes-suitcase.jpg" alt="How to fold clothes to save space in luggage: carry-on suitcase packed with rolled T-shirts and jeans, a packing cube and shoes in a cloth bag" class="wp-image-1189" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-07-rolled-clothes-suitcase.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-07-rolled-clothes-suitcase-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-07-rolled-clothes-suitcase-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fold-clothes-07-rolled-clothes-suitcase-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">11. Combine folding with packing cubes</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Packing cubes keep each type of clothing together and stop rolls from shifting. File fold shirts into one cube and roll underwear and socks into another. Put shoes in a cloth bag so they do not dirty your clothes.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">12. Fold for seasonal storage</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For out-of-season clothes, fold them loosely so creases do not set, and store them clean and dry in a lidded bin. Clear sides help you see what is inside. Slide bins under the bed or onto a high shelf; our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-bedroom-storage-ideas/">small bedroom storage ideas</a> and <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/storage-ideas-for-small-spaces/">storage ideas for small spaces</a> cover the best spots.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common Folding Mistakes That Waste Space</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Folding to the wrong height.</strong> Too tall and the drawer will not close; too short and the rows flop over.</li>


<li><strong>Stacking instead of filing.</strong> Stacks hide most of your clothes and collapse when you pull from the middle.</li>


<li><strong>Overstuffing the drawer.</strong> You should be able to remove one item easily; if not, it is time for a <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/closet-clean-out/">closet clean-out</a>.</li>


<li><strong>Folding damp laundry.</strong> Clothes that are not fully dry can smell musty or mildew in a closed drawer.</li>


<li><strong>Balling socks.</strong> It stretches the cuffs and takes more room than folding them flat.</li>

</ul>



